36239b76-4b24-4058-8e57-f84cd20acc89	Mathematics	Number Systems	9	\N	easy	mcq	Which of the following is an irrational number?	{"A": "$\\\\sqrt{4}$", "B": "$\\\\sqrt{2}$", "C": "$0.\\\\overline{3}$", "D": "$\\\\frac{22}{7}$"}	B	An irrational number cannot be expressed as $p/q$ with integer $p,q$ and $q\\neq 0$, and its decimal expansion is non-terminating, non-recurring. $\\sqrt{4}=2$ is rational, $0.\\overline{3}=1/3$ is rational, and $22/7$ is rational by definition. $\\sqrt{2}=1.41421356\\ldots$ is non-terminating and non-recurring, so it is irrational. (NCERT Class 9 Ch. 1.2)	\N	\N	4	t	\N	2026-05-06 07:31:05.567576	2026-05-06 07:31:05.567576	Class 9 Maths Ch. 1 Number Systems	\N	\N	\N	\N	{FOUNDATION}	MANUAL	approved	en
a6c89f9b-5fa3-43b9-a863-291095710970	Mathematics	Number Systems	9	\N	medium	mcq	The value of $(\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{2})$ is	{"A": "$3$", "B": "$7$", "C": "$\\\\sqrt{10}$", "D": "$2\\\\sqrt{10}$"}	A	Using the identity $(a+b)(a-b)=a^2-b^2$, we get $(\\sqrt{5})^2-(\\sqrt{2})^2 = 5-2 = 3$. This is a standard rationalisation pattern used to remove surds from products. (NCERT Class 9 Ch. 1.4 Operations on Real Numbers)	\N	\N	4	t	\N	2026-05-06 07:31:05.567576	2026-05-06 07:31:05.567576	Class 9 Maths Ch. 1 Number Systems	\N	\N	\N	\N	{FOUNDATION}	MANUAL	approved	en
281656a1-20a6-4090-a1d0-b5c31ef7a9fd	Mathematics	Polynomials	9	\N	easy	mcq	The degree of the polynomial $4x^3 - 2x^5 + 7x - 1$ is	{"A": "$3$", "B": "$4$", "C": "$5$", "D": "$1$"}	C	The degree of a polynomial in one variable is the highest power of the variable with a non-zero coefficient. The terms are $4x^3, -2x^5, 7x, -1$ with powers $3, 5, 1, 0$. The highest is $5$, so the degree is $5$. (NCERT Class 9 Ch. 2.2)	\N	\N	4	t	\N	2026-05-06 07:31:05.567576	2026-05-06 07:31:05.567576	Class 9 Maths Ch. 2 Polynomials	\N	\N	\N	\N	{FOUNDATION}	MANUAL	approved	en
